## Summary

pnpm v12 resolved `pnpm add <pkg>` without a version to `latest` even when the `tag` setting named another dist-tag. pnpm v11 threads `tag` as `defaultTag` into `parseWantedDependencies` (`pnpm11/installing/deps-installer/src/install/installSome.ts`) and into the resolution context (`createResolutionContext.ts`), so a version-less `npm:` alias or `jsr:` dependency resolves it too.

This PR:

- adds `tag` to `Config`, read from `pnpm-workspace.yaml`, the global `config.yaml`, `PNPM_CONFIG_TAG`, and `--config.tag`, plus `Config::default_tag()` (falls back to `latest` when unset or empty);
- resolves a brand-new version-less `add` with a non-default tag the way `add <pkg>@<tag>` does, so the version is locked as well (this builds on the picked-version locking from pnpm/pnpm#17240, which has merged);
- uses the same default for a version-less `jsr:` add and in the install resolver's `default_tag`. A `jsr:` add also seeds the version its tag picked, under the `@jsr/...` registry name, so the lockfile holds that version and not the highest one in the saved range;
- reads an empty version (`pnpm add foo@`, `"foo": ""` in `package.json`) as no version, so it resolves the tag too. v11 does the same in `parseWantedDependencies.ts` and `resolveNpm.ts`;
- keeps `pnpm dlx` / `pnx` on `latest`: `configure_cache_install` clears `tag`, matching v11's `resolveDlxPackages`. Without this, a project `tag` changed what dlx ran, and the dlx cache, keyed on the unresolved specs, then se
